        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "69c7c9fd-03d2-50fd-9c17-56c2bf99cce8",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Arthur Russell, Jerry Harrison, Tony Bongiovi, Lance Quinn\n- Bands: Hecuba, Talking Heads\n- Works: Wild Combination, Psycho Killer\n- Places: Los Angeles\n\nFacts:\n- Arthur Russell was born Charles Arthur Russell, Jr. on May 21, 1951, and he was an American cellist, composer, producer, singer, and musician whose work spanned many styles.\n- An acoustic b-side version of “Psycho Killer” featured Arthur Russell on cello.\n- In the 1992 liner notes for \"\", Jerry Harrison wrote that he was glad Tony Bongiovi and Lance Quinn were persuaded that the version with cellos should not be the only one.\n- Arthur Russell died on April 4, 1992.\n- Hecuba performed at the Los Angeles premiere of Wild Combination, a film about Arthur Russell, and performed their versions of Russell's music.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "71f0b1ed-744a-50c5-94d1-fddd79564728",
        "text": "Page: Hecuba (band). Sentence: They performed at the Los Angeles premier of \"Wild Combination\": a film about Arthur Russell (musician) performing their versions of Russell's music.\nPage: Arthur Russell (musician). Sentence: Arthur Russell (born Charles Arthur Russell, Jr.; May 21, 1951 – April 4, 1992) was an American cellist, composer, producer, singer, and musician whose work spanned a disparate range of styles.\nPage: Psycho Killer. Sentence: In the liner notes for \"\" (1992), Jerry Harrison wrote of the b-side of the single, an acoustic version of the song that featured Arthur Russell on cello, \"I'm glad we persuaded Tony [Bongiovi] and Lance [Quinn] that the version with the cellos shouldn't be the only one.\"",
